1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,786
One thought you have when stocks rise
can wreck your account in the next crash.

2
00:00:05,166 --> 00:00:08,685
Mr. Lee grew his account to 100
million won with a single stock,

3
00:00:08,685 --> 00:00:10,225
and people called him a pro.

4
00:00:10,605 --> 00:00:13,785
4 years later, that 100 million
won had become 32 million won.

5
00:00:14,165 --> 00:00:17,852
And that's while the S&P
500 rose more than 50%.

6
00:00:18,232 --> 00:00:20,356
What broke Mr. Lee
wasn't the crash itself,

7
00:00:20,356 --> 00:00:22,677
but the thoughts he had
every time stocks rose.

8
00:00:23,056 --> 00:00:27,831
Facing the five thoughts that came
to Mr. Lee, choose what you would do.

9
00:00:28,211 --> 00:00:31,944
Each time you choose, just count
whether you thought the same way he did.

10
00:00:32,324 --> 00:00:34,474
In the shoes of Mr.
Lee, the so-called pro,

11
00:00:34,474 --> 00:00:36,825
you'll choose, starting
with the first thought.

12
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:41,471
A year ago, you put all
your savings into one stock.

13
00:00:41,851 --> 00:00:46,161
It rose nearly 5x in a year, and
your account hit 100 million won.

14
00:00:46,541 --> 00:00:49,769
That's because COVID boosted
remote work and online classes,

15
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:51,921
and a video-call company's stock soared.

16
00:00:52,301 --> 00:00:55,581
The S&P 500 rose 16% that same year.

17
00:00:55,961 --> 00:00:59,795
People call you a stock pro
and ask you for your next pick.

18
00:01:00,175 --> 00:01:04,825
So, do you keep this stock, saying
you made the money by picking well?

19
00:01:05,205 --> 00:01:07,803
Or do you sell part of
it to lock in your gains,

20
00:01:07,803 --> 00:01:10,077
saying you just got lucky riding the mood?

21
00:01:10,457 --> 00:01:11,397
Make your choice.

22
00:01:11,777 --> 00:01:13,957
3, 2, 1.

23
00:01:14,337 --> 00:01:19,721
Here, the right choice is to call it luck
and sell part of it to lock in your gains.

24
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:25,101
The date was December 31, 2020, and the
stock was the video-call company Zoom.

25
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:28,724
Had you kept it like Mr.
Lee, believing it was skill,

26
00:01:28,724 --> 00:01:31,356
Zoom would then have
fallen as much as 84%.

27
00:01:31,736 --> 00:01:34,635
Even 4 years later,
you'd still be down 76%.

28
00:01:35,015 --> 00:01:37,625
Of course, some really
do profit through skill.

29
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:41,955
But skill or luck shows up not when
stocks rise, but when they fall.

30
00:01:42,335 --> 00:01:47,954
Of the companies in today's S&P
500, 14 rose more than 2x in 2020.

31
00:01:48,334 --> 00:01:51,838
9 of them saw their stock price
cut in half within 2 years.

32
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:55,718
Mistake luck for skill, and you'll
bet even bigger on one stock.

33
00:01:56,098 --> 00:01:59,177
In the end, the right choice
is to see big gains as luck,

34
00:01:59,177 --> 00:02:01,553
and sell part of the
winner to lock them in.

35
00:02:02,553 --> 00:02:07,231
6 weeks later, the stock rose again,
and your account grew another 28%.

36
00:02:07,611 --> 00:02:11,423
You're wondering where to put the 28
million won you made in the meantime.

37
00:02:11,803 --> 00:02:16,463
You're eyeing a fitness equipment maker
whose stock rose more than 5x in a year.

38
00:02:16,843 --> 00:02:21,000
Home workouts were all the rage, and
its indoor bikes sold like hotcakes.

39
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,673
The internet is full of
posts about buying stocks

40
00:02:23,673 --> 00:02:25,920
like this with gains and
multiplying them again.

41
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:31,799
So, do you guard your gains like principal
and not buy this fitness equipment maker?

42
00:02:32,179 --> 00:02:34,537
Or do you buy this
company with that money,

43
00:02:34,537 --> 00:02:36,512
saying it's fine to lose your gains?

44
00:02:36,892 --> 00:02:37,832
Make your choice.

45
00:02:38,212 --> 00:02:40,392
3, 2, 1.

46
00:02:40,772 --> 00:02:43,437
Here, the right choice
is to guard your gains

47
00:02:43,437 --> 00:02:46,102
like your principal and
not buy this company.

48
00:02:46,482 --> 00:02:51,306
The date was February 12, 2021, and the
fitness equipment maker was Peloton.

49
00:02:51,686 --> 00:02:55,274
Had you bought Peloton with
your gains like Mr. Lee,

50
00:02:55,274 --> 00:02:58,241
95% would have vanished
by the end of 2022.

51
00:02:58,621 --> 00:03:01,992
Even at the end of 2024,
you'd still be down 94%.

52
00:03:02,372 --> 00:03:06,635
Of course, gains feel like money you
never had, so losing them hurts less.

53
00:03:07,015 --> 00:03:11,355
But the money in your account, gains
or principal, is all equally yours.

54
00:03:11,735 --> 00:03:16,851
Money that has lost 94% must rise
nearly 18x to get back to where it was.

55
00:03:17,231 --> 00:03:19,691
In the end, the right
choice is to treat gains

56
00:03:19,691 --> 00:03:22,311
like principal and not
bet them on trendy stocks.

57
00:03:23,311 --> 00:03:28,152
Seven months later, your stock
is down 54% from its peak.

58
00:03:28,532 --> 00:03:31,377
You're thinking of selling
all of it and moving

59
00:03:31,377 --> 00:03:34,040
the money into a big
company everyone knows.

60
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:37,850
That company's stock rose
nearly 2x in a year and a half.

61
00:03:38,230 --> 00:03:41,630
Subscribers to its new streaming
service are growing fast, too.

62
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:44,647
People say a company like
this will never go under,

63
00:03:44,647 --> 00:03:46,716
so it's safe to put it all in one place.

64
00:03:47,096 --> 00:03:50,075
So, do you split the money
across several companies,

65
00:03:50,075 --> 00:03:52,596
since even a big company
can be cut in half?

66
00:03:52,976 --> 00:03:55,684
Or do you put all the
money into this company,

67
00:03:55,684 --> 00:03:58,038
since a big name everyone knows is safe?

68
00:03:58,418 --> 00:03:59,358
Make your choice.

69
00:03:59,738 --> 00:04:01,918
3, 2, 1.

70
00:04:02,298 --> 00:04:05,074
Here, the right choice
is to spread your money,

71
00:04:05,074 --> 00:04:07,378
since even a big company can be halved.

72
00:04:07,758 --> 00:04:11,762
The date was September 30, 2021,
and the company was Disney.

73
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:15,632
Had you put all your Zoom
money into Disney like Mr. Lee,

74
00:04:15,632 --> 00:04:18,143
it would then have fallen as much as 53%.

75
00:04:18,523 --> 00:04:21,828
Even at the end of 2024,
you'd still be down 33%.

76
00:04:22,208 --> 00:04:25,653
Of course, big companies don't
go under as easily as small ones.

77
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:30,783
But a company surviving and its stock
not falling are two different things.

78
00:04:31,163 --> 00:04:36,956
The next year, in 2022, Meta fell as
much as 74%, and Amazon as much as 51%.

79
00:04:37,336 --> 00:04:41,429
Being a famous name doesn't make one
company safe enough to put everything in.

80
00:04:41,809 --> 00:04:45,661
In the end, the right choice is to
know big companies can fall hard too,

81
00:04:45,661 --> 00:04:46,999
and not go all in on one.

82
00:04:47,999 --> 00:04:53,161
Three months later, at year-end, the S&P
500 had set 70 new record highs that year.

83
00:04:53,541 --> 00:04:57,961
The news says markets are flush with
cash and predicts more gains next year.

84
00:04:58,341 --> 00:05:00,789
You're thinking of
borrowing 20 million won

85
00:05:00,789 --> 00:05:03,180
on a credit line to buy more of the index.

86
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,800
Your math: the index will rise
more than the loan interest.

87
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:09,969
So, do you borrow to
buy more of the index,

88
00:05:09,969 --> 00:05:12,498
saying it won't fall much anytime soon?

89
00:05:12,878 --> 00:05:15,938
Or do you not borrow, saying
that after rising this much,

90
00:05:15,938 --> 00:05:17,388
it could fall hard anytime?

91
00:05:17,768 --> 00:05:18,708
Make your choice.

92
00:05:19,088 --> 00:05:21,268
3, 2, 1.

93
00:05:21,648 --> 00:05:26,576
Here, the right choice is to accept it
could fall hard anytime, and not borrow.

94
00:05:26,956 --> 00:05:29,025
The date was December 31, 2021,

95
00:05:29,025 --> 00:05:32,762
and three days later the index
peaked and began to fall.

96
00:05:33,142 --> 00:05:35,544
Had you borrowed to buy more like Mr. Lee,

97
00:05:35,544 --> 00:05:39,032
that money would have shrunk
24% by October of the next year.

98
00:05:39,412 --> 00:05:42,753
Getting back to even would
have taken nearly 2 years,

99
00:05:42,753 --> 00:05:45,022
with interest going out every month.

100
00:05:45,402 --> 00:05:48,847
Without debt, all you'd have
had to do was ride out the drop.

101
00:05:49,227 --> 00:05:53,363
Of course, the index did rise again in the
end, so borrowing to buy may seem fine.

102
00:05:53,743 --> 00:05:56,468
But borrowed money comes
with interest and a due date,

103
00:05:56,468 --> 00:05:58,689
which makes holding on
in a drop far harder.

104
00:05:59,069 --> 00:06:04,165
We looked at the 716 times the S&P
500 hit a record high since 1990.

105
00:06:04,545 --> 00:06:08,714
One time in three, it fell more than 10%
below that day's level within 2 years.

106
00:06:09,094 --> 00:06:12,734
The longer record highs keep coming, the
more you should plan ahead for a big drop.

107
00:06:13,114 --> 00:06:15,312
In the end, the right
choice is to know stocks

108
00:06:15,312 --> 00:06:17,557
can fall even in a rally,
and not take on debt.

109
00:06:18,557 --> 00:06:21,387
A month later, the stock
you sold four months

110
00:06:21,387 --> 00:06:23,902
earlier is at just over 1/4 of its peak.

111
00:06:24,282 --> 00:06:26,413
Online forums are full
of posts saying that,

112
00:06:26,413 --> 00:06:28,496
compared with the peak,
it's a bargain now.

113
00:06:28,876 --> 00:06:33,215
You're thinking of pulling your money
out of the index to buy this stock again.

114
00:06:33,595 --> 00:06:38,711
So, do you not buy it back, saying the
peak was a price made by a passing mood?

115
00:06:39,091 --> 00:06:44,059
Or do you buy it back with money from the
index, saying the peak was its true worth?

116
00:06:44,439 --> 00:06:45,379
Make your choice.

117
00:06:45,759 --> 00:06:47,939
3, 2, 1.

118
00:06:48,319 --> 00:06:53,441
Here, the right choice is to see the peak
as a passing mood, and not buy it back.

119
00:06:53,821 --> 00:06:57,828
The date was January 31, 2022,
and the stock was Zoom once again.

120
00:06:58,208 --> 00:07:01,213
Had you bought it again like
Mr. Lee because it looked cheap,

121
00:07:01,213 --> 00:07:03,232
it would then have fallen as much as 64%.

122
00:07:03,612 --> 00:07:07,042
Even at the end of 2024,
you'd still be down 47%.

123
00:07:07,422 --> 00:07:11,596
Of course, a stock that has fallen far
from its peak can't help looking cheap.

124
00:07:11,976 --> 00:07:16,454
But that peak may have been a price
set by the mood when everyone piled in.

125
00:07:16,834 --> 00:07:21,534
To get from 1/4 of the peak back
to the peak, it has to rise 3.7x.

126
00:07:21,914 --> 00:07:24,921
Zoom is still at just over
1/6 of its peak today.

127
00:07:25,301 --> 00:07:29,421
In the end, the right choice is not to
judge cheap or expensive by the peak.

128
00:07:30,421 --> 00:07:33,831
In this test, how many times
did you think like Mr. Lee?

129
00:07:34,211 --> 00:07:39,527
If never, you stay calm when stocks rise
and prepare for crashes in advance: Pass.

130
00:07:39,907 --> 00:07:42,148
If once or twice, you know how to prepare,

131
00:07:42,148 --> 00:07:45,137
but your thinking wavers when
you're in profit: Caution.

132
00:07:45,517 --> 00:07:47,555
If three times or more, like Mr. Lee,

133
00:07:47,555 --> 00:07:50,749
you're likely to end up losing
even when you profit: Fail.

134
00:07:51,749 --> 00:07:55,515
As this test shows, better safe
than sorry: the more stocks rise,

135
00:07:55,515 --> 00:07:59,977
the more preparing for a crash in advance
is the way to get rich with stocks.

136
00:08:00,357 --> 00:08:02,567
We wish you all successful investing.

137
00:08:02,947 --> 00:08:04,477
Thank you for watching.
